00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 26.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.000
It's hard to see through people's heads, isn't it?

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:41.360
Impossible! Anyway, to answer your question, I thought it was really interesting, especially

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.919
what the speaker said about the job market.

00:01:43.919 --> 00:01:49.300
Me too. I mean, we know we're going into a really competitive field so it's obvious

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.480
that we may struggle to get work.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:56.239
That's right. And we know we can't all have that dream job.

00:01:56.239 --> 00:02:01.239
Yeah, but it looks like there's a whole range of areas of work that we hadn't even

00:02:01.239 --> 00:02:04.680
thought of, like fashion journalism for instance.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.679
Yeah, I wasn't expecting so many career options.

00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:12.839
Hmm. Overall, she had quite a strong message, didn't she?

00:02:12.839 --> 00:02:19.240
She did. She kept saying things like, I know you all think this, but… and then she'd

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:20.960
tell us how it really is.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:24.880
Perhaps she thinks students are a bit narrow-minded about the industry.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.880
It was a bit harsh though. We know it's a tough industry.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:32.880
Yeah. And we're only first years after all. We've got a lot to learn.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:39.880
Exactly. Do you think our secondary school education should have been more career-focused?

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:45.880
Well, we had numerous talks on careers, which was good, but none of them were very inspiring.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.880
They could have asked more people like today's speaker to talk to us.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:55.880
I agree. We were told about lots of different careers, just when we needed to be, but not

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.880
by the experts who really know stuff.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.880
So, did today's talk influence your thoughts on what career you'd like to take up in the future?

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:09.880
Well, I promised myself that I'd go through this course and keep an open mind till the end.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:15.880
But I think it's better to pick an area of the industry now and then aim to get better and better at it.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:19.880
Well, I think we'll just have to differ on that issue.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:25.880
One thing's for certain though. From what she said, we'll be unpaid assistants in the industry for quite a long time.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:26.880
Hmm.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.880
I'm prepared for that. Aren't you?

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.880
Actually, I'm not going to accept that view.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.880
Really? But she knows it's the case. And everyone else says the same.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:40.880
That doesn't mean it has to be true for me.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.880
OK. Well, I hope you're right.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:53.880
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 27 to 30.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:04:18.880
Now listen and answer questions 27 to 30.

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:23.880
I thought the speaker's account of her first job was fascinating.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.880
Yeah. She admitted she was lucky to get work being a personal dresser for a musician.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.880
She didn't even apply for the job and there she was getting paid to choose all his clothes.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:42.880
It must have felt amazing. Though she said all she was looking for back then was experience, not financial reward.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:48.880
Hmm. And then he was so mean telling her she was more interested in her own appearance than his.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.880
But she did realise he was right about that, which really made me think.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:01.880
I'm always considering my own clothes, but now I can see you should be focusing on your client.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.880
She obviously regretted losing the job.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:09.880
Well, as she said, she should have hidden her negative feelings about him, but she didn't.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:17.880
It was really brave the way she picked herself up and took that job in retail. Fancy working in a shop after that?

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.880
Yeah, well, she recommended we all do it at some point.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:29.880
I guess as a designer you'd get to find out some useful information, like how big or small the average shopper is.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.880
I think that's an issue for manufacturers, not designers.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.880
However, it would be useful to know if there's a gap in the market, you know?

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:41.880
An item that no one's stocking, but that consumers are looking for.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:47.880
Yeah, people don't give up searching. They also take things back to the store if they aren't right.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:56.880
Yeah. Imagine you worked in an expensive shop and you found out the garments sold there were being returned because they fell apart in the wash.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:59.880
Yeah, it would be good to know that kind of thing.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:00.880
Yeah.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:10.880
That is the end of Part 3. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 3.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:35.880
.

